1
00:00:00,029 --> 00:00:03,559
In Core, Inventory separates the item
catalogue from what

2
00:00:03,559 --> 00:00:05,239
the department currently holds.

3
00:00:05,690 --> 00:00:10,420
On hand provides Items and Location
filters, while Unit and Person lookups

4
00:00:10,420 --> 00:00:12,449
help identify a particular holder.

5
00:00:12,909 --> 00:00:17,049
Start by choosing the right item and
location before interpreting a quantity.

6
00:00:17,530 --> 00:00:21,309
This walkthrough follows one fictional
training consumable through recorded

7
00:00:21,309 --> 00:00:24,590
software movements, rather than claiming
that anyone physically

8
00:00:24,590 --> 00:00:26,739
inspected or transported supplies.

9
00:00:26,939 --> 00:00:31,280
The Items tab lists catalogue entries and
their Tracking mode and Status.

10
00:00:31,709 --> 00:00:33,910
New item opens the catalogue workflow.

11
00:00:34,459 --> 00:00:39,239
On How it is tracked, this example uses By
quantity with Track lots

12
00:00:39,270 --> 00:00:41,270
and Expiration required selected.

13
00:00:41,690 --> 00:00:44,379
Controlled substance and Kit item remain
off.

14
00:00:44,820 --> 00:00:48,009
Decide how an item should be tracked
before stock exists;

15
00:00:48,379 --> 00:00:50,070
later changes can be restricted.

16
00:00:50,500 --> 00:00:54,170
Industrial teams can apply the same
distinction to bulk consumables

17
00:00:54,240 --> 00:00:56,019
and individually tracked equipment.

18
00:00:56,189 --> 00:01:00,080
The Review page brings the prepared item
together: Academy training

19
00:01:00,080 --> 00:01:03,560
batteries, measured in packs, with its
code and description.

20
00:01:03,920 --> 00:01:09,439
It also shows minimum stock five, reorder
point eight, and the fictional unit cost.

21
00:01:09,819 --> 00:01:11,779
Read these values before Save.

22
00:01:12,180 --> 00:01:16,280
A catalogue entry describes the item; it
does not itself receive twenty

23
00:01:16,280 --> 00:01:18,510
packs or prove a physical purchase.

24
00:01:18,879 --> 00:01:23,399
The next screens use that item in an
existing saved lot and stock movement.

25
00:01:23,549 --> 00:01:26,799
Lots identifies batches separately from
quantities.

26
00:01:27,200 --> 00:01:31,639
The saved ACADEMY lot A row belongs to
Academy training batteries and

27
00:01:31,639 --> 00:01:33,919
displays a November thirtieth expiry.

28
00:01:34,330 --> 00:01:37,910
New lot is available for creating another
batch, but this view

29
00:01:37,910 --> 00:01:39,469
demonstrates the saved row.

30
00:01:39,899 --> 00:01:43,560
Check the lot and expiry when recording a
movement so that later stock

31
00:01:43,560 --> 00:01:45,800
history refers to the intended batch.

32
00:01:46,159 --> 00:01:48,800
Creating a lot alone does not add stock.

33
00:01:49,030 --> 00:01:52,960
In Adjust stock, the prepared Review shows
Receive, the training

34
00:01:52,960 --> 00:01:57,270
item and lot, Station as the destination,
and Amount twenty.

35
00:01:57,730 --> 00:02:00,829
Its note clearly identifies a software
exercise.

36
00:02:01,249 --> 00:02:04,619
Read the complete movement and choose
Record movement only when

37
00:02:04,619 --> 00:02:06,259
it matches the intended receipt.

38
00:02:06,769 --> 00:02:10,359
The filtered On hand result then shows
twenty packs at Station.

39
00:02:10,849 --> 00:02:14,679
That stored balance is the result of the
receipt, rather than proof supplied

40
00:02:14,679 --> 00:02:16,390
by the preparation screen alone.

41
00:02:16,589 --> 00:02:21,779
New transfer reviews the same item and
lot, with Station as Source location, Unit

42
00:02:21,810 --> 00:02:24,780
as Destination location, and Amount five.

43
00:02:25,200 --> 00:02:27,789
Confirm both ends before Record movement.

44
00:02:28,219 --> 00:02:32,879
A generic location label does not identify
a particular apparatus by itself.

45
00:02:33,359 --> 00:02:36,910
The Transfers list then shows the saved
Station-to-Unit move.

46
00:02:37,420 --> 00:02:41,570
Search and rescue or wildland crews can
use this pattern for a documented

47
00:02:41,570 --> 00:02:45,609
restock, while keeping software movement
distinct from actual delivery.

48
00:02:45,760 --> 00:02:49,870
History shows the recorded Receive of
twenty and Transfer of five

49
00:02:49,919 --> 00:02:51,120
for the training batteries.

50
00:02:51,560 --> 00:02:55,850
The transfer appears as one visible row
with Station pointing to Unit,

51
00:02:56,150 --> 00:02:57,930
and its note explains the purpose.

52
00:02:58,390 --> 00:03:01,669
Use the item and location filters to
understand a change.

53
00:03:02,130 --> 00:03:05,960
Attest as witness and Rebuild stock
balances are separate controls;

54
00:03:06,380 --> 00:03:10,010
this example reads the ledger without
invoking either operation.

55
00:03:10,219 --> 00:03:13,350
Issue equipment reviews two packs from
Station for Matthew

56
00:03:13,350 --> 00:03:17,040
Casey, together with the expected return
date and a training note.

57
00:03:17,440 --> 00:03:20,269
Confirm the person and quantity before
Issue.

58
00:03:20,870 --> 00:03:25,790
The Issuances result shows Personnel, two
outstanding, and a Return action.

59
00:03:26,219 --> 00:03:30,889
The named recipient was established in the
review; this table uses the holder label.

60
00:03:31,250 --> 00:03:35,059
Community response and security teams can
use that distinction to track

61
00:03:35,059 --> 00:03:36,819
supplies signed out to a person.

62
00:03:36,989 --> 00:03:41,070
A later saved Issuances view shows
Partially returned and

63
00:03:41,159 --> 00:03:42,869
one pack still outstanding.

64
00:03:43,249 --> 00:03:47,420
This is a partial return, not a completed
return of everything issued.

65
00:03:47,800 --> 00:03:51,249
The Return action remains available for
the outstanding balance.

66
00:03:51,630 --> 00:03:54,679
Here we review that stored result; the
intervening

67
00:03:54,679 --> 00:03:56,730
return-entry dialog is not shown.

68
00:03:57,169 --> 00:04:01,150
Check the remaining quantity and status
instead of treating any return as

69
00:04:01,150 --> 00:04:03,169
evidence that the whole issue is closed.

70
00:04:03,389 --> 00:04:07,760
At this point in the example, the balance
table shows fourteen packs at Station,

71
00:04:07,999 --> 00:04:10,370
five at Unit, and one at Personnel.

72
00:04:10,890 --> 00:04:15,809
Those twenty packs reconcile the receipt,
transfer, issue and partial return.

73
00:04:16,290 --> 00:04:20,150
These are the quantities in this dated
snapshot, before later usage.

74
00:04:20,470 --> 00:04:24,019
In your own department, read the current
filtered balances and exact

75
00:04:24,019 --> 00:04:27,589
holder context rather than copying the
demonstration quantities.

76
00:04:27,709 --> 00:04:31,209
Counts, alerts and reports contains
Inventory alerts.

77
00:04:31,610 --> 00:04:34,949
This captured row is a Low stock alert for
the training batteries,

78
00:04:35,149 --> 00:04:37,700
marked Resolved across All locations.

79
00:04:38,110 --> 00:04:42,299
The page also describes expiry and
overdue-return alerts, but no

80
00:04:42,299 --> 00:04:44,249
expiring-lot result is shown here.

81
00:04:44,729 --> 00:04:47,319
Refresh alerts is a separate available
action.

82
00:04:47,700 --> 00:04:52,229
Read the alert type and status alongside
stock data; a resolved alert does

83
00:04:52,229 --> 00:04:54,069
not replace a current stock review.

84
00:04:54,400 --> 00:04:59,439
Inventory reports opens Choose a report
with Inventory on hand displayed.

85
00:04:59,900 --> 00:05:04,900
Continue leads toward Narrow it down, but
this view is the chooser, not

86
00:05:04,900 --> 00:05:07,450
a generated report or exported PDF.

87
00:05:07,860 --> 00:05:12,529
Fire and EMS administrators, like other
services, should verify a report’s

88
00:05:12,529 --> 00:05:14,889
scope before relying on its numbers.

89
00:05:15,309 --> 00:05:20,180
Keep catalogue setup, recorded movements,
stock counts and reporting distinct

90
00:05:20,379 --> 00:05:24,520
so that another person can understand what
each result actually establishes.

91
00:05:24,920 --> 00:05:29,120
The same process supports fire and EMS,
community paramedicine and

92
00:05:29,120 --> 00:05:32,789
mental health response, search and rescue,
security departments and

93
00:05:32,789 --> 00:05:37,659
companies, wildland contractors, and
industrial emergency response teams.

